  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:20:38.780Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn individual earning purely compensation income from a single employer in the Philippines is not required to file an annual income tax return if the employer has withheld the correct tax.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, with correct tax withholding, are exempt from filing an annual income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A of the NIRC explicitly allows for substituted filing of income tax returns for employees whose income tax has been correctly withheld by their employer. The certificate of withholding issued by the employer, which is stamped 'received' by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR), serves as the equivalent of an income tax return for these employees.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor individuals in this situation, the requirement to file an annual income tax return is effectively waived, simplifying their tax compliance obligations. They can rely on the withholding certificate provided by their employer as proof of tax compliance, reducing administrative burdens and potential filing errors.",
